      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The higher index of suspicion refers mainly to the difficulty in visual identification of certain conditions such as melanoma on darker skin tones rather than a difference in disease behavior. Clinicians are advised to be vigilant because the typical visual markers, such as easily observable changes in asymmetry, border irregularity, and color variation seen in lighter skin, are less apparent which can lead to delayed diagnosis. While there's some research into the progression of diseases potentially varying among ethnicities, there isn't conclusive evidence to support significant behavioral differences or aggressiveness. Providers' cautious approach pertains to achieving early detection and removing uncertainties in the critical initial stages of diagnosis. Emphasis is on expanding diagnostic awareness across all skin tones to mitigate potential risks and reduce disparities.",
